        "text": "The key to determining your portfolio strategy lies in how you want to manage your risk. There are many kinds of risks you might encounter while investing in trading. The first is market risk, the potential loss you experience if the asset loses value. The second is liquidity risk. This occurs in illiquid markets where you cannot easily find buyers for your assets. This can also cause potential losses if you cannot sell at the price you want. Next is operational risk, where potential losses stem from operational failures, such as human error, hardware or software failure, or intentional fraudulent conduct. Finally, we have systemic risk, including possible losses caused by the collapse of an entire industry, market, or financial system. One example would be Lehman Brothers bankruptcy in 2007 and its effect on the global financial industry.",
        "start": 320,
        "end": 47560,
